-- Q1440: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The federal program name, CFDA number, awarding agency, and expenditure amount for each program","Only the total amount of federal awards received during the fiscal year","Only those programs with individual expenditures exceeding $750,000","A comprehensive list of all state and local grants the entity received"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.510), the SEFA must include the federal program name, Assistance Listing (formerly CFDA) number, the federal awarding agency, and the amount expended for each program. The schedule covers all federal awards and reconciles to the financial statements.
Wrong (B): Reporting only a total amount would not satisfy the Uniform Guidance requirement for program-level detail including names, CFDA numbers, and individual expenditures.
Wrong (C): The SEFA must include all federal programs, not just those exceeding the $750,000 Type A threshold.
Wrong (D): The SEFA reports federal awards only; state and local grants are outside its scope.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1440;

-- Q1441: Audit Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To communicate the planned audit approach to the client''s audit committee","To evaluate the results of completed substantive testing procedures","To document the detailed procedures for each individual account balance","To set the scope, timing, and direction of the audit engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 300.07, the overall audit strategy sets the scope, timing, and direction of the audit and guides development of the more detailed audit plan. It addresses resource deployment, amounts allocated to specific areas, and how resources are managed and supervised.
Wrong (A): Communicating with the audit committee is a separate requirement under AU-C 260, not the primary purpose of the overall audit strategy.
Wrong (B): Evaluating substantive test results occurs during fieldwork and completion, not during strategy development.
Wrong (C): Documenting detailed procedures for each account is the function of the audit plan under AU-C 300.09, not the overall strategy.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1441;

-- Q1447: Audit Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The engagement letter level, as specified in the terms of engagement","The financial statement level only, matching overall materiality","The management representation letter level for final assertions","The individual account balance or class of transactions level"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 320.A12, tolerable misstatement is applied at the individual account balance or class of transactions level. It represents performance materiality applied to a particular sampling procedure and helps the auditor determine sample sizes and evaluate results under AU-C 530.
Wrong (A): The engagement letter documents the terms of the audit engagement under AU-C 210; it is not a level at which tolerable misstatement is applied.
Wrong (B): Overall materiality under AU-C 320.04 is applied at the financial statement level; tolerable misstatement operates below that threshold at the account level.
Wrong (C): The management representation letter is obtained at audit completion under AU-C 580 and has no connection to the application of tolerable misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1447;

-- Q1450: Audit Planning [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Establish the engagement fee based on internal audit staffing and coverage","Evaluate whether the entity actually needs to maintain an internal audit function","Replace external audit procedures entirely with the internal audit work","Determine whether internal audit work can modify external audit procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 610.09, the auditor evaluates the internal audit function during planning to determine whether its work can be used to modify the nature, timing, or extent of external audit procedures. This assessment considers the internal auditors'' competence, objectivity, and systematic approach under AU-C 610.12.
Wrong (A): Audit fees are a business matter and are not determined by the scope of internal audit coverage.
Wrong (B): Advising on whether the entity needs an internal audit function is a management decision, not an audit planning objective.
Wrong (C): AU-C 610.05 confirms the external auditor retains sole responsibility for the opinion and cannot replace external procedures entirely with internal audit work.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1450;

-- Q1454: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Determining the audit fees for the current year engagement","Assessing risks of material misstatement and planning audit procedures","Deciding whether to withdraw from the current engagement entirely","Preparing the engagement letter for the following year''s audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.12, a change in the entity''s revenue recognition policy directly impacts risk assessment and planned audit procedures. AU-C 315.A60 requires the auditor to evaluate whether the change is appropriate, properly applied, and adequately disclosed. AU-C 330.07 then requires designing further procedures responsive to those assessed risks.
Wrong (A): Audit fee determination is a business matter and is not the primary consideration when an accounting policy changes.
Wrong (C): A change in accounting policy does not automatically warrant withdrawal from the engagement; the auditor should evaluate and respond to the resulting risks.
Wrong (D): The engagement letter for the following year is a future matter and is not the primary planning consideration for a current-year policy change.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1454;

-- Q1463: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Obtain the predecessor auditor''s complete set of working papers","Determine the audit fee that the predecessor charged the client","Obtain information bearing on the decision to accept the engagement","Transfer responsibility for the prior year audit to the successor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 210 and AU-C 510, the successor auditor communicates with the predecessor to obtain information bearing on the acceptance decision, including reasons for the change, disagreements with management about accounting policies or audit procedures, and any concerns about management integrity. This communication requires client permission.
Wrong (A): Obtaining working papers may occur after engagement acceptance; it is not the primary purpose of pre-acceptance communication with the predecessor.
Wrong (B): The predecessor''s audit fee is a business matter irrelevant to the successor''s acceptance decision.
Wrong (D): Responsibility for the prior year audit remains with the predecessor; it is not transferable through successor-predecessor communication.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1463;

-- Q1465: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GAAP prohibits all transactions between related parties entirely","The auditor must pre-approve all related party transactions before execution","Related party transactions are always presumed to be fraudulent in nature","Related party transactions may lack arm''s-length terms and cause misstatement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 550.06, the auditor considers related party relationships during planning because such transactions may not be conducted at arm''s-length prices or equivalent terms. This could result in material misstatement of amounts or inadequate disclosure. AU-C 550.A10 explains that the economic substance may differ from legal form.
Wrong (A): GAAP does not prohibit related party transactions; it requires appropriate disclosure under ASC 850.
Wrong (B): The auditor has no authority to approve or block management''s business transactions with related parties.
Wrong (C): Related party transactions are not automatically fraudulent; the concern is the risk of non-arm''s-length terms that could cause material misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1465;

-- Q1469: Audit Planning [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Materiality can only be increased, never decreased, during the audit","Materiality revisions require the client''s written approval to proceed","Materiality should be revised when new information would have changed it initially","Materiality is fixed during planning and cannot be changed during fieldwork"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 320.12, the auditor should revise materiality for the financial statements as a whole if information comes to the auditor''s attention during the audit that would have caused a different amount to be established initially. Materiality can be revised upward or downward based on changed circumstances.
Wrong (A): AU-C 320 permits materiality to be revised in either direction—increased or decreased—depending on the information obtained during fieldwork.
Wrong (B): Materiality is a matter of auditor judgment; client approval is not required for the auditor to revise materiality.
Wrong (D): AU-C 320.12 explicitly requires the auditor to consider revising materiality when new information warrants it, not to treat it as fixed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1469;

-- Q1470: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the quality of the internal auditors'' work through sufficient procedures","Reperform every procedure the internal auditor has already completed","Obtain and verify the internal auditor''s professional license number","Issue a joint audit report together with the internal audit function"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 610.12–.14, when the external auditor uses internal auditors'' work as audit evidence, the external auditor must perform sufficient procedures to evaluate the quality and objectivity of that work. This includes examining items tested, reperforming certain procedures, and evaluating whether conclusions are appropriate.
Wrong (B): Reperforming all work would eliminate any efficiency gained from using internal audit; the external auditor performs selected procedures on the body of work.
Wrong (C): Professional licensing status is not a required evaluation criterion under AU-C 610; the standard focuses on competence, objectivity, and systematic approach.
Wrong (D): The external auditor retains sole responsibility for the audit opinion under AU-C 610.05 and does not issue joint reports with internal audit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1470;

-- Q1471: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Refuse to audit the investment property account balance entirely","Hire a separate specialist to redo the entire fair value valuation","Accept the specialist''s work as audit evidence without further evaluation","Evaluate the specialist''s competence, objectivity, and work appropriateness"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 500.08, when management uses a specialist, the auditor must evaluate the specialist''s competence, capabilities, and objectivity, and assess whether the work is appropriate audit evidence. The auditor considers the source data, assumptions used, and methods employed by the specialist.
Wrong (A): Refusing to audit the account is inappropriate; the auditor should evaluate the specialist''s work and design procedures to obtain sufficient evidence.
Wrong (B): Hiring a separate specialist to redo the entire valuation is not required; the auditor evaluates whether management''s specialist''s work is appropriate.
Wrong (C): Automatically accepting the specialist''s work without evaluation would fail to meet AU-C 500 requirements for assessing the reliability of audit evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1471;

-- Q1476: Audit Planning [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Materiality applies only to the balance sheet, not other statements","Materiality levels for different statements must be approved by the audit committee","The same materiality level must be applied uniformly across all financial statements","Different materiality levels may be set for each financial statement based on users"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 320.10, the auditor may determine that different materiality levels are appropriate for different financial statements. Users may be more sensitive to misstatements in the income statement than the balance sheet, requiring a lower materiality for income statement items. The auditor uses the lowest applicable materiality to drive procedures.
Wrong (A): Materiality applies to all financial statements, not just the balance sheet; AU-C 320 does not restrict its application to a single statement.
Wrong (B): Setting materiality levels is a matter of professional judgment for the auditor; audit committee approval is not required under AU-C 320.
Wrong (C): AU-C 320.10 explicitly permits different materiality levels for different statements when user sensitivity differs across statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1476;

-- Q1478: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the engagement but pursue alternative procedures for predecessor information","Decline the engagement because predecessor communication is absolutely mandatory","Accept the engagement without any additional procedures since communication is optional","Report the dissolved predecessor firm to the state board of accountancy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 210 and AU-C 510, while predecessor communication is ordinarily required, circumstances such as firm dissolution may prevent it. The successor auditor should consider alternative procedures—reviewing prior year financial statements, SEC filings, or regulatory reports—to obtain sufficient information for acceptance and planning.
Wrong (B): Predecessor communication is not an absolute prerequisite; the auditor can accept the engagement when alternative procedures provide adequate information despite inability to communicate.
Wrong (C): Accepting without any additional procedures ignores the auditor''s responsibility to obtain information normally gathered through predecessor communication.
Wrong (D): Reporting the dissolved firm to the state board is not a required step and does not address the successor''s need to gather acceptance-relevant information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1478;

-- Q1479: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Map the structure, identify intercompany transactions, and assess elimination risk","Accept management''s consolidation schedules without performing independent testing","Audit only the parent company''s standalone financial statements for efficiency","Reduce materiality to zero for all intercompany transactions and balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 600, complex organizational structures with numerous intercompany transactions require the auditor to map the structure, identify significant intercompany balances and transactions, and assess the risk that elimination entries may be misstated or incomplete. Improper eliminations can materially misstate consolidated financial statements.
Wrong (B): Accepting consolidation schedules without testing would fail to address the risk of misstated elimination entries and violates AU-C 500 evidence requirements.
Wrong (C): Auditing only the parent''s standalone statements would ignore material intercompany transactions and component-level risks that affect consolidated results.
Wrong (D): Reducing materiality to zero is impractical and unnecessary; the auditor should assess risk and design procedures at an appropriate materiality level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1479;

-- Q1480: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assess fraud risk implications, evaluate anti-corruption programs, and plan FCPA procedures","Ignore jurisdiction-specific risks if the entity maintains a code of ethics","Limit the audit scope to domestic operations and exclude foreign locations","Refuse the engagement because the jurisdictional corruption risk is too high"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 240.17, operations in high-corruption jurisdictions require the auditor to consider jurisdiction-specific fraud risks during planning. This includes evaluating management''s anti-corruption compliance programs, assessing FCPA or similar regulatory violation risks, and planning procedures to address bribery and corruption risks.
Wrong (B): A code of ethics alone does not mitigate jurisdiction-specific corruption risks; the auditor must still evaluate the effectiveness of anti-corruption controls.
Wrong (C): Excluding foreign operations from audit scope would be inappropriate when those operations are significant and carry elevated fraud risk.
Wrong (D): Elevated jurisdictional risk does not automatically require declining the engagement; the auditor should assess and respond to the risk with appropriate procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1480;

-- Q1481: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Dismiss the concern because revenue recognition is always a presumed fraud risk","Evaluate the concern based on the specific facts, complexity, and circumstances","Remove the associate from the engagement for questioning the partner''s judgment","Immediately issue a going concern opinion based on the revenue complexity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 240.27, while revenue recognition is a presumed fraud risk, the specific complexity of multiple performance obligations warrants separate evaluation as a significant risk. The engagement partner should evaluate the facts, consider whether additional procedures are needed beyond the presumed fraud risk response, and document the assessment.
Wrong (A): Dismissing the concern because revenue is already a presumed risk ignores that specific complexities may require additional risk assessment and tailored procedures under AU-C 315.
Wrong (C): Removing a team member for raising a valid concern would violate professional standards encouraging open discussion of fraud risks during planning under AU-C 240.15.
Wrong (D): A going concern opinion relates to the entity''s ability to continue operating, not to the complexity of revenue arrangements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1481;

-- Q1482: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Decision to change the financial reporting framework used by the entity","Requirement to report the prior year restatement to regulatory authorities","Decision to set the audit fee higher than the amount charged in the prior year","Assessment of risk of material misstatement and planned procedures for inventory"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.12, a prior year restatement due to inventory valuation errors directly impacts the auditor''s current-year risk assessment. The auditor would likely assess higher risk of material misstatement for inventory, plan more extensive substantive procedures, and evaluate whether the underlying cause has been remediated.
Wrong (A): The auditor does not decide the financial reporting framework; that is management''s responsibility, and a restatement does not trigger a framework change.
Wrong (B): Reporting a restatement to regulators is management''s obligation, not a direct effect on the auditor''s planning procedures.
Wrong (C): Audit fee decisions are business matters unrelated to the auditor''s risk assessment response to a prior year restatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1482;

-- Q1483: Audit Planning [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Time zone differences between component audit locations across jurisdictions","Language differences affecting the preparation of audit documentation","The cost of international travel required to visit component locations","Differences in reporting frameworks, auditing standards, and regulatory requirements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 600.11, the most significant challenge in multi-jurisdiction group audits involves differences in financial reporting frameworks, auditing standards, and regulatory requirements across jurisdictions. These differences affect the nature and extent of component auditor work, the type of report issued, and the need for reconciling procedures.
Wrong (A): Time zone differences are logistical inconveniences but do not fundamentally affect the nature and sufficiency of audit evidence obtained.
Wrong (B): Language differences may complicate documentation review but are not the most significant audit challenge compared to substantive standards differences.
Wrong (C): Travel cost is an administrative concern that does not affect the quality or scope of audit procedures performed across jurisdictions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1483;

-- Q1484: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Realizability of deferred tax assets, including future taxable income and valuation allowance","Accepting management''s assertion that the deferred tax assets are fully realizable","Requiring write-off of all deferred tax assets as a condition of engagement acceptance","Ignoring deferred tax assets entirely because they represent non-cash balance items"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 540.08, deferred tax assets require careful evaluation of realizability, which involves significant judgment. The auditor must plan procedures to assess whether sufficient future taxable income exists, evaluate the nature and timing of temporary differences, consider tax planning strategies, and determine whether a valuation allowance is needed under ASC 740.
Wrong (B): Accepting management''s assertion without testing would fail to provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence, especially for estimates requiring significant judgment.
Wrong (C): Requiring write-off of all deferred tax assets as an engagement condition would be inappropriate and overstep the auditor''s role; realizability is evaluated through audit procedures.
Wrong (D): Deferred tax assets are recognized balance sheet items requiring audit attention regardless of their non-cash nature.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1484;

-- Q1485: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Understand the claim, evaluate management''s assessment, and plan inquiry of legal counsel","Ignore the lawsuit because litigation outcomes are inherently uncertain and unpredictable","Immediately include a going concern paragraph in the current audit report","Advise the client to settle the lawsuit before the audit fieldwork begins"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 501.12, significant pending litigation represents a contingent liability requiring the auditor to understand the nature of the claim, evaluate management''s assessment of probable outcomes, and plan procedures including sending a letter of audit inquiry to the entity''s legal counsel. Proper accrual and disclosure must be evaluated under ASC 450.
Wrong (B): Ignoring the lawsuit would violate the auditor''s responsibility to obtain sufficient evidence about contingent liabilities that could result in material misstatement.
Wrong (C): Including a going concern paragraph is premature without first evaluating the litigation''s potential impact on the entity''s financial position and viability.
Wrong (D): Advising the client to settle litigation is a business decision outside the auditor''s role; the auditor''s responsibility is to evaluate and report on the financial statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1485;

-- Q1486: Audit Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the engagement since predecessor communication is merely a professional courtesy","Report the prospective client to the appropriate regulatory authorities immediately","Accept the engagement but plan to issue a qualified opinion from the outset","Consider this refusal a scope limitation and evaluate management integrity implications"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 210.10, a client''s refusal to permit predecessor communication is a significant factor the auditor should carefully evaluate. The auditor should consider the implications regarding management integrity and whether this limitation affects the ability to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence for acceptance.
Wrong (A): Predecessor communication is a professional requirement under AU-C 210, not merely a courtesy; the refusal has substantive implications for acceptance.
Wrong (B): Reporting the client to regulators is not required based solely on a refusal to permit predecessor communication.
Wrong (C): Planning to issue a qualified opinion before beginning fieldwork is premature; the auditor should first evaluate whether the engagement can be accepted at all.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1486;

-- Q1487: Audit Planning [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the going concern assessment related to the entity''s future viability","Only the inventory valuation for assets at the closed plant locations","Only the staffing plan and resource allocation for the audit engagement","Risk assessment across multiple areas including impairment, charges, and disclosures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.11, major restructuring affects multiple financial statement areas and requires comprehensive risk assessment. The auditor must evaluate asset impairment, restructuring charge estimates, employee severance and benefit obligations, lease termination costs, disclosure adequacy, and potential going concern implications.
Wrong (A): Limiting the evaluation to going concern alone would miss restructuring charges, asset impairment, severance obligations, and disclosure requirements also affected.
Wrong (B): Focusing only on inventory valuation ignores other significant areas such as long-lived asset impairment, restructuring liabilities, and employee benefit obligations.
Wrong (C): The staffing plan is an administrative matter; the auditor must first assess how restructuring affects the risk of material misstatement across financial statement areas.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1487;

-- Q1498: Risk Assessment [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Relates pervasively to the financial statements as a whole across many assertions","Relates specifically to a single account balance or individual class of transactions","Can be fully addressed through a single focused substantive test procedure","Is always caused by intentional fraud rather than error or poor judgment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A105, financial statement-level risks relate pervasively to the financial statements as a whole and potentially affect many assertions. Management integrity concerns and aggressive corporate culture are examples. AU-C 330.05 requires overall responses such as assigning more experienced staff and increasing supervision.
Wrong (B): Assertion-level risks relate to specific accounts or transactions, not the pervasive, organization-wide concerns described in the scenario.
Wrong (C): Pervasive financial statement-level risks cannot be addressed by a single test; they require overall responses affecting the audit as a whole.
Wrong (D): Financial statement-level risks may arise from error, poor judgment, or weak control environments—not exclusively from fraud.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1498;

-- Q1500: Risk Assessment [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Relying entirely on internal controls over the mineral rights valuation","Issuing a disclaimer of opinion on the financial statements as a whole","Decreasing the overall extent of substantive procedures for the assertion","Designing more effective substantive procedures or testing closer to year-end"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.07, when inherent risk is assessed as high for a particular assertion, the auditor must design more effective substantive procedures to reduce detection risk to an acceptably low level. This may include performing procedures closer to period-end, using larger sample sizes, or selecting more effective procedure types such as external confirmations.
Wrong (A): Relying entirely on internal controls is inappropriate when inherent risk is high; substantive procedures are required under AU-C 330.
Wrong (B): Issuing a disclaimer is a reporting decision and is not an appropriate response to high inherent risk when the auditor can design responsive substantive procedures.
Wrong (C): Decreasing substantive procedures when inherent risk is high would increase detection risk beyond acceptable levels under AU-C 200.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1500;

-- Q1503: Risk Assessment [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Assign more experienced staff, increase supervision, and add unpredictability","Only increase sample sizes for the specific substantive tests performed","Eliminate all analytical procedures from the planned audit approach entirely","Reduce the total number of assertions tested across the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 330.05, when risks are assessed at the financial statement level, the auditor designs overall responses addressing the audit as a whole. AU-C 330.A1 identifies appropriate responses including assigning more experienced staff, increasing supervision, incorporating unpredictability, and making general changes to the nature, timing, or extent of procedures.
Wrong (B): Increasing sample sizes for specific accounts addresses assertion-level risk, not the pervasive financial statement-level risks described in the scenario.
Wrong (C): Eliminating analytical procedures would reduce audit evidence and is the opposite of strengthening the overall audit approach in response to elevated risk.
Wrong (D): Reducing the number of assertions tested would weaken the audit response when pervasive risks require a more comprehensive approach.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1503;

-- Q1505: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the client''s explanation for the control deficiency without further action","Withdraw from the engagement due to the ineffective internal controls","Issue a qualified opinion on internal controls in the audit report immediately","Increase assessed control risk and design responsive substantive procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.07, when controls are not operating effectively, the auditor increases the assessed level of control risk and must modify planned substantive procedures to compensate. AU-C 330.A14 indicates the auditor may increase sample sizes, perform procedures closer to year-end, or change the nature of procedures from analytical to detailed testing.
Wrong (A): Accepting the client''s explanation without further action would result in insufficient audit evidence when controls have failed to operate effectively.
Wrong (B): Withdrawing from the engagement is premature; the auditor should first design responsive substantive procedures to address the increased risk.
Wrong (C): Immediately issuing a modified opinion is premature; the auditor should first perform additional substantive procedures to determine whether material misstatement exists.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1505;

-- Q1506: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Routine monthly depreciation entries recorded by the accounting system","Standard payroll accrual entries posted at each period-end close","Non-standard period-end entries with unusual account combinations","Recurring monthly entries for fixed operating expenses like rent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240.32, the auditor must test journal entries and other adjustments for evidence of management override of controls. The auditor focuses on non-standard entries, particularly those made near the end of the reporting period, with unusual account combinations, by unexpected individuals, or with round-dollar amounts.
Wrong (A): Routine depreciation entries are systematic, automated, and predictable, making them low-risk for management override.
Wrong (B): Standard payroll accruals follow a recurring pattern and are typically subject to established controls, making them unlikely vehicles for override.
Wrong (D): Recurring entries for fixed expenses like rent are routine and predictable, posing minimal management override risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1506;

-- Q1509: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Excluding goodwill from audit scope because it involves an accounting estimate","Performing only analytical procedures on the recorded goodwill balance","Understanding related controls and performing substantive procedures for the risk","Accepting management''s goodwill impairment analysis without independent testing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.15, for significant risks the auditor must obtain an understanding of the entity''s controls relevant to the risk and perform substantive procedures specifically responsive to it. For goodwill valuation, AU-C 540.13 requires evaluating management''s impairment analysis, testing key assumptions, and AU-C 620.05 permits engaging a valuation specialist.
Wrong (A): Excluding goodwill from audit scope is inappropriate; significant risks require focused substantive procedures, not scope exclusion.
Wrong (B): Performing only analytical procedures would not provide sufficiently persuasive evidence for a significant risk under AU-C 330.15.
Wrong (D): Accepting management''s analysis without independent evaluation provides insufficient evidence when goodwill valuation has been identified as a significant risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1509;

-- Q1510: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the breach''s impact on data integrity and assess additional misstatement risks","Ignore the breach because cybersecurity is an IT issue unrelated to the audit","Rely solely on management''s own assessment of the cybersecurity breach impact","Immediately withdraw from the audit engagement without further evaluation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.11–.12, the auditor must identify and assess risks of material misstatement by understanding the entity and its environment, including events affecting financial statements. A cybersecurity breach could compromise data integrity, introduce unauthorized transactions, or impair automated controls. AU-C 315.A30 requires considering business risks that may result in misstatement.
Wrong (B): Cybersecurity breaches affecting financial reporting systems directly impact the reliability of financial data and internal controls, making them relevant to the audit.
Wrong (C): Relying solely on management''s assessment without independent evaluation would fail to provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence about the breach''s impact.
Wrong (D): Withdrawing from the engagement is premature without first evaluating the severity and financial reporting implications of the breach.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1510;

-- Q1511: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the transaction was at arm''s length, its purpose, and misstatement risk","Only whether the related party transaction is disclosed in the financial footnotes","Whether the related party entity is publicly traded or privately held","Only the dollar amount of the transaction without considering its nature"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 550.18, when assessing risk for related party transactions, the auditor considers whether the transaction was conducted at arm''s length, the business purpose and economic substance, and whether the terms could result in material misstatement or inadequate disclosure. AU-C 550.A27 emphasizes that transactions outside the normal course of business present higher risk.
Wrong (B): Evaluating only footnote disclosure ignores the substance and terms of the transaction, which are critical to assessing misstatement risk under AU-C 550.
Wrong (C): Whether the related party is publicly traded does not address the nature, purpose, or arm''s-length character of the specific transaction.
Wrong (D): Evaluating only the dollar amount without considering the transaction''s nature, purpose, and terms would not satisfy risk assessment obligations under AU-C 550.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1511;

-- Q1512: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue a scope limitation on the accounts receivable audit procedures","Eliminate all substantive procedures for the accounts receivable balance","Set detection risk low by performing more extensive substantive procedures","Accept a higher level of detection risk to reduce the audit effort required"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330.07, the audit risk model requires that when the combined risk of material misstatement is high, the auditor must set detection risk at a low level. AU-C 330.A18 explains this means performing more persuasive substantive procedures, increasing sample sizes, using more reliable evidence sources, or testing closer to the balance sheet date.
Wrong (A): A scope limitation is a reporting matter, not an appropriate response to high assessed risk; the auditor should design more extensive substantive procedures.
Wrong (B): Eliminating substantive procedures would provide no audit evidence for accounts receivable and violate AU-C 330 requirements.
Wrong (D): Accepting higher detection risk when the risk of material misstatement is already high would result in unacceptably high overall audit risk under the audit risk model.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1512;
